The second part, `else n * factorial(n - 1)`, is the recursive case. It multiplies the current `n` by the result of calling the same function on `n - 1`. So for `factorial(5)`, you get 5 times `factorial(4)`, which is 4 times `factorial(3)`, and so on down to `factorial(1)`, which returns 1, and the whole chain collapses into 5 times 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, which is 120.

